<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Nice Weather Today!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's Raining.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b><br>
            Listening: Receptive: Follow very simple directions and instructions.<br>
            Interactive: Respond to simple conversations on familiar topics by using gestures or by pointing.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b><br>
            Listening: Can follow basic instructions related to weather (e.g., "Look outside, it's sunny.").<br>
            Can recognize weather-related words like "sunny" or "raining" in conversations.<br>
            Can respond to questions about the weather (e.g., "What is the weather today?").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and starts with a simple "Hello!" song. Then, the teacher holds up realia (e.g., a small toy sun, a cloud drawing, a pretend umbrella) or large, colorful flashcards depicting "sunny", "raining", "cloudy", and "windy" weather. The teacher asks, "What's the weather today?" and points outside or to a window, encouraging students to look. </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher shows the flashcard for "sunny," says "Sunny!" clearly, models the /s/ sound, and makes a gesture for the sun. The teacher repeats this for "raining" (modeling /r/ sound and gesture like falling rain), "cloudy" (gesture like moving clouds), and "windy" (gesture like blowing wind). The teacher pronounces the initial /w/ sound in "weather" clearly. Students listen and imitate gestures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses simple questions and gestures to check understanding.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding up "raining" flashcard) "Is it sunny?" (Students shake head or point to "raining").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding up "sunny" flashcard) "Is this 'raining' or 'sunny'?" (Students point to "sunny").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Show me 'windy'!" (Students make the 'windy' gesture).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] [5-7 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Nice Weather Today!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, simple audio track with natural dialogues and sounds, accompanied by large, clear picture cards or a digital presentation showing scenes of different weather (sunny park, rainy street, cloudy sky, windy day). The audio consists of very short sentences like "Look outside! It is sunny." "Oh no, it is raining!" "It is cloudy today." "The wind is blowing. It is windy." The teacher uses flashcards for "sun", "rain", "clouds", "wind".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening to descriptions of simple weather conditions (e.g., "It is sunny.") and basic questions about weather (e.g., "What is the weather today?") to engage in simple weather-related conversations. Pragmatic: Talking about weather and asking questions about weather.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays the audio. Students listen carefully. After each sentence, the teacher pauses and asks students to point to the correct flashcard/picture. E.g., Teacher plays "It is sunny." then asks, "Point to 'sunny'!"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays the full audio again. This time, the teacher holds up two contrasting flashcards (e.g., "sunny" and "raining") and asks, "Is it sunny or raining?" Students listen and point to the correct picture.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] [10-12 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice listening for and identifying weather conditions using target vocabulary (sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy, weather, sun, rain, clouds, wind) and grammar (Present continuous: "It is raining"; Subject + verb + adjective: "It is sunny"; WH-questions: "What is the weather today?"). Focus on distinguishing /s/ in "sunny" and /r/ in "raining" and the initial /w/ in "weather" through listening.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Listen and Point:</b> The teacher places weather flashcards (sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy) on the board or floor. The teacher says a sentence, "It is sunny today!" or "It is raining outside!", and students listen and point to the corresponding flashcard. The teacher intentionally emphasizes the /s/ and /r/ sounds in the words.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice: Listen and Move (TPR):</b> The teacher assigns a simple action for each weather word (e.g., "sunny" = smile and put hands up like the sun; "raining" = wiggle fingers down; "cloudy" = hunch shoulders; "windy" = sway side to side). The teacher calls out a weather word or sentence (e.g., "It is windy!"), and students listen and perform the action. This reinforces listening comprehension through physical response.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction / Peer Check (Listen and Respond):</b> Students work in pairs, each with a set of mini weather flashcards. One student (Student A) closes their eyes or turns away. The other student (Student B) holds up a card. The teacher then says "What is the weather today?" Student B listens and points to the card, then Student A opens their eyes and checks if Student B pointed correctly. This is very basic, focused on listening to the question and pointing response.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] [15-18 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Nice Weather Today!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> "Weather Command Game" (TPR-based listening comprehension). Students stand up. The teacher gives various instructions related to weather, and students listen and perform the corresponding actions or point to parts of the classroom where weather images might be placed.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher places large weather pictures (sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy) around the classroom.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher gives commands like: "Everybody, point to 'sunny'!" "If it's raining, pretend to open an umbrella!" "Show me your 'windy' dance!" "What is the weather today? Point outside if it's sunny!"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher ensures a mix of instructions targeting different weather words and responses using gestures and pointing. The activity focuses on students' ability to listen to the instruction and respond non-verbally or with very minimal one-word utterances (e.g., "Sun!").</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in listening comprehension (understanding commands quickly), collaborative interaction (responding as a group), and communicative task completion (successfully following all instructions).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] [8-10 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher conducts a quick "Listen and Circle" activity. Students are given a worksheet with four pictures, each depicting a different weather condition (sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy). The teacher orally states, "Circle 'raining'," then "Circle 'sunny'." Students listen and circle the correct picture. The teacher circulates and observes student responses.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student correctly follows at least 3 out of 4 simple instructions related to weather words (e.g., "Circle 'sunny'").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student recognizes and responds to "What is the weather today?" by pointing to the correct weather picture or making the appropriate gesture.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student demonstrates comprehension of the /s/ sound in "sunny" and /r/ sound in "raining" by distinguishing them in listening tasks.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students are asked to show:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Thumbs up if I know "sunny" and "raining."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Thumbs down if I need more help with weather words.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] [5 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What new weather words did I listen to and understand today? (e.g., "sunny," "raining")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Was it easy or difficult to follow the teacher's instructions about the weather?</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson to engage students and improve their listening skills? (e.g., the TPR activities, the flashcards, the audio input).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Which weather words or grammatical structures (e.g., "It is raining.") were most challenging for students to understand receptively?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds might be needed for students struggling with listening comprehension in the next lesson? (e.g., more visual cues, slower pronunciation, repetition).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 2, students will engage in Reading Comprehension using the same core weather vocabulary introduced today. We will review the visual representation of the weather words to prepare them for recognizing these words in written form.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] [2-3 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>